pr_body:
This issue was discoevered while I was testing SSL_new_from_listener() using a newly created unit test. It has turned out the QUIC stack at few places contain pattern as follows:
	foo(QUIC_WHATEVER *q, BIO_ADDR *a)
	{
	   q->a = *a;
	}

The problem is that derefencning a that way is risky. If the address `a` comes from BIO_lookup_ex() it may actually be shorter than sizeof(BIO_ADDR). Using BIO_ADDR_copy() is the right thing to do here.

Fixes: #26241

pr_comments:
New version reverts the changes to `ssltraceref.txt`, because they were wrong. The test failure we've seen was caused by false positive mismatch in `ossl_qtx_write_pkt()` here:
```
 816     was_coalescing = (qtx->cons != NULL && qtx->cons->data_len > 0);
 817     if (was_coalescing)
 818         if (!addr_eq(&qtx->cons->peer, pkt->peer)
 819             || !addr_eq(&qtx->cons->local, pkt->local)) {
 820             /* Must stop coalescing if addresses have changed */
 821             ossl_qtx_finish_dgram(qtx);
 822             was_coalescing = 0;
 823         }
```
The false-positive mismatch in `addr_eq()` was caused by `BIO_ADD_copy()` which copies just correct amount of bytes which represent address. it left all remaining bytes in destination buffer (of `BIO_ADDR` type) uninitialized.

adding a `memset()` to `BIO_ADDR_make()` makes the destination address buffer safe for `addr_eq()`. Another option is to make `addr_eq()` smarter so it won't rely on `memcmp()`.

issue_context.body:
The code snippet below is incorrect because it may read more than what's actually available in buffer which holds address.
```
239
240             if (!BIO_lookup_ex(dst_host, dst_port_str, BIO_LOOKUP_CLIENT,
241                                AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0, &bai)) {
242                 fprintf(stderr, "[ Server ] BIO_lookup_ex(%s, %s) error (%s)\n",
243                         dst_host, dst_port_str,
244                         ERR_reason_error_string(ERR_get_error()));
245                 goto done;
246             }
247
248             if (!SSL_set1_initial_peer_addr(ssl_qconn, BIO_ADDRINFO_address(bai))) {
249                 fprintf(stderr, "[ Server ] SSL_new_from_listener error (%s)\n",
250                         ERR_reason_error_string(ERR_get_error()));
251                 goto done;
252             }
253
```
at line 253 we call `BIO_lookup_ex()` which is a wrapper around `getaddrinfo()` in libc. The returns sets `bai` which is openssl's wrapper of libc's `struct addrinfo` it looks like this:
```
     struct addrinfo {
             int ai_flags;           /* input flags */
             int ai_family;          /* address family for socket */
             int ai_socktype;        /* socket type */
             int ai_protocol;        /* protocol for socket */
             socklen_t ai_addrlen;   /* length of socket-address */
             struct sockaddr *ai_addr; /* socket-address for socket */
             char *ai_canonname;     /* canonical name for service location */
             struct addrinfo *ai_next; /* pointer to next in list */
     };
```
at line 248 we use `BIO_ADDRINFO_address()` which dereferences bai and gives `ai_addr` member. However this is too optimistic for `ossl_quic_conn_set_initial_peer_add()` (a workhorse for `SSL_set1_initial_peer_addr()`): 
```
1243 int ossl_quic_conn_set_initial_peer_addr(SSL *s,
1244                                          const BIO_ADDR *peer_addr)
1245 {
1246     QCTX ctx;
1247
1248     if (!expect_quic_cs(s, &ctx))
1249         return 0;
1250
1251     if (ctx.qc->started)
1252         return QUIC_RAISE_NON_NORMAL_ERROR(&ctx, ERR_R_SHOULD_NOT_HAVE_BEEN_CALLED,
1253                                        NULL);
1254
1255     if (peer_addr == NULL) {
1256         BIO_ADDR_clear(&ctx.qc->init_peer_addr);
1257         return 1;
1258     }
1259
1260     ctx.qc->init_peer_addr = *peer_addr;
1261     return 1;
1262 }
```
The problem is `BIO_ADDR` is defined as union:
```
18 union bio_addr_st {
 19     struct sockaddr sa;
 20 #  if OPENSSL_USE_IPV6
 21     struct sockaddr_in6 s_in6;
 22 #  endif
 23     struct sockaddr_in s_in;
 24 #  ifndef OPENSSL_NO_UNIX_SOCK
 25     struct sockaddr_un s_un;
 26 #  endif
 27 };
```
and there is no guarantee ai->ai_addrlen is always equal to sizeof(BIO_ADDR). I believe in the most common situations this is actually less than that, so line 1260 reads past `peer_addr` buffer.

there are two options to fix it:
  - let `SSL_set1_initial_peer_addr()` to accept `BIO_ADDRINFO`
  - or add size_t/socklen_t argument so `ossl_quic_conn_set_initial_peer_addr()` know how much memory to copy.
